readings.c1s1:
¶
II. ''intrans.'' 1. To jerk or throw one's about; roll or tumble about; be restless or easy; fling.

{{smaller block|To ''toss'' and fling, and to be restless, only frets and rages our pain.
{{right|''Tillo''}}
Sohrab alone, he slept not; all night long
He had lain wakeful, ''tossing'' on his bed.
{{right|''M. Arnold'', Sohrab and Rust}}}}

2. To be flung or rocked about; be kept motion.

{{smaller block|Your mind is ''tossing'' on the ocean.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., i.}}
We left behind the painted buoy
That ''tosses'' at the harbor-mouth.
{{right|''Tennyson'', The Voy}}}}

3. Same as ''to toss up'' (which see, below).

{{smaller block|They spend their time and what money they may ha ''tossing'' for beer, till they are either drunk or pennile
{{right|''Mayhew'', London Labour and London Poor, II.}}}}

'''To toss up''', to throw up a coin, and decide somethin the side turned up when it falls.

{{smaller block|He ''tossed up'' whether he should hang or drown. coin fell on its edge in the clay, and saved his life for time.
{{right|''J. Ashton'', Social Life in Reign of Queen Anne, I}}
The catcher of the senior nine ''tossed up'', and the ju were sent to the bat.
{{right|''St. Nicholas'', XVII.}}}}

'''toss''' (tos), ''n.'' [< ME. ''toss'' (rare); < ''toss'', ''v.''] A sudden fling or jerk; especially, a qu movement of the head backward or upwar

{{smaller block|There is hardly a polite sentence in the following logues which does not absolutely require some . . . able ''toss'' of the head.
{{right|''Swift'', Polite Conversation,}}
Anon, with ''toss'' of horn and tail, . . .
They leap some farmer's broken pale.
{{right|''Whittier'', The Dro}}}}

readings.c1s2:
2. A pitch; a throw: as, the ''toss'' of a ball coin.—3. The distance over which one to anything; a throw.

{{smaller block|No 2 Brick Court, Middle Temple, . . . was but cuit ''toss'' from Crown Office Row.
{{right|''B. E. Martin'', Footprints of Charles Lan}}}}

4. A state of agitation or excitement; a c motion.

{{smaller block|Lord! what a ''tosse'' I was for some time in, that could not justly tell where it [the buried gold] was.
{{right|''Pepys'', Diary, Oct. 10,}}}}

{{smaller block|“We are all in a ''toss'' in our neighborhood,” said tress Pottle.
{{right|''S. Judd'', Margaret,}}}}

5. A toss-up: with reference to a case in wl chance decides.

{{smaller block|One of the most earnest advocates of the measure “'Tis the ''toss'' of a copper.” ''The Century'', XXXVIII.}}

6. The mow or bay of a barn into which g is put preparatory to threshing. ''Halli'' [Prov. Eng.]—'''Pitch and toss.''' {{smaller|See ''pitch-and''}} —'''To win the toss''', {{smaller|to win in a case decided by the ing up of a coin; hence, in general, to have luck on side; gain the day.}}

{{smaller block|Lordynges, now ye have herd
Off these tounes hou it ferd;
How Kyng Richard with his maystry
''Wan the toss'' off Sudan Turry.
{{right|''Richard Coer de Lion'' (Weber's Metr. Rom., II.}}}}

{{smaller block|Hasn't old Brooke ''won the toss'', with his lucky halfpe and got choice of goals?
{{right|''T. Hughes'', Tom Brown at Rugby,}}}}

'''tossel'''<sup>1</sup> (tos′l), ''n.'' An obsolete or dialectal f of ''tassel''<sup>1</sup>.

'''tossel'''<sup>2</sup> (tos′l), ''n.'' In ''arch.'', same as ''torsel''. G

'''tosser''' (tos′ėr), ''n.'' [< ''toss'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One or that which tosses: as, a ''tosser'' of balls.

readings.c2s1:
¶
{{smaller block|What is the use of counting on any success of mine? It is a mere ''toss up'' whether I shall ever do more than keep myself decently. {{right|''George Eliot'', Middlemarch, lxxxiii.}}}}

{{smaller block|"He'll do," said the Doctor quietly. "It must have been a ''toss-up'' all through the night." {{right|''R. Kipling'', Only a Subaltern.}}}}

'''tossy''' (tos′i), ''a.'' [< ''toss'' + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] Tossing; especially, tossing the head as in scorn or contempt; hence, affectedly indifferent; pert; contemptuous. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|Argemone answered by some ''tossy'' commonplace. {{right|''Kingsley'', Yeast, vii. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''tossy-tail''' (tos′i-tāl), ''adv.'' Topsyturvy. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]

'''tost''' (tost). Another spelling of ''tossed'', preterit and past participle of ''toss''.

'''tostamente''' (tos-tȧ-men′te), ''adv.'' [It., < ''tosto'', quick, bold.] In ''music'', quickly; rapidly. [Rare.]

'''tostart†''', ''v. i.'' [ME. ''tosterten''; < ''to-''<sup>2</sup> + ''start''<sup>1</sup>.] To start or spring apart; burst.

{{smaller block|Lo, myn herte,<br>It spredeth so for joie, it wol ''tosterte.'' {{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, ii. 980.}}}}

'''tosticated, tossicated''' (tos′ti-, tos′i-kā-ted), ''a.'' [A reduction of ''intoxicated'', and confused, in def. 2, with ''toss'', ''tossed'', ''tost''.] 1. Intoxicated. [Colloq.]—2. Tossed about; restless; perplexed. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|I have been so ''tosticated'' about since my last that I could not go on in my journal manner. {{right|''Swift'', Journal to Stella, xlviii.}}}}

readings.c2s4:
¶
{{smaller block|These ''totted'' together will make a pretty beginning of my little project.
{{right|''H. Brooke'', Fool of Quality, II. 211. (''Davies.'')}}}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|It is a temporary, not a ''total'' retreat, such as we may leave off or resume.
{{right|''Bp. Atterbury'', On Mat. xiv. 23.}}}}

4†. Summary; concise; curt.

{{smaller block|Do you mean my tender ears to spare,
That to my questions you so ''total'' are?
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'' (Arber's Eng. Garner, I. 549).}}}}

{{smaller|'''Constructive total loss.''' See ''constructive.''—'''Total abstinence,''' entire abstinence from intoxicants.—'''Total cause.''' See ''cause'', 1.—'''Total curvature, degree, depravity, differential, differentiation.''' See the nouns.—'''Total earth.''' Same as ''dead earth'' (which see, under ''earth''<sup>1</sup>).—'''Total eclipse,''' an eclipse in which the whole surface of the eclipsed luminary is obscured.—'''Total method, ophthalmoplegia, part, residual, term,''' etc. See the nouns.—'''Total reflection.''' See ''refraction'', 1. =Syn. 1-3. ''Whole, Entire'', etc. See ''complete.''}}

II. ''n.'' The whole; the whole sum or amount; an aggregate.

{{smaller block|A tapster's arithmetic may soon bring his particulars . . . to a ''total.''
{{right|''Shak.'', T. and C., i. 2. 124.}}}}

¶
'''total''' (tō′tal), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''totaled, totalled'', ppr. ''totaling, totalling''. [< ''total'', ''n.''] 1. To bring to a total; accumulate; sum; add: sometimes with ''up''.

{{smaller block|The sum 365 is correct when ''totalled;'' but the mode in which it is obtained is vitiated by two anomalies.
{{right|''N. and Q.'', 7th ser., XI. 135.}}}}

{{smaller block|Prices, numbers, and dates are all clearly tabulated and ''totalled up'' for us.
{{right|''The Engineer'', LXV. 467.}}}}

2. To reach a total of; amount to.

{{smaller block|86 small craft, . . . ''totalling'' 500 tons, were built of wood.
{{right|''The Engineer'', LXV. 6.}}}}

'''totalis†''', ''n.'' [ML. ''totalis'', in ''summa totalis'', the sum total: see ''total.''] The sum total; the whole amount.

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|Cast your eye only upon the ''totalis'', and no further; for to traverse the bill would betray you to be acquainted with the rates of the market.
{{right|''Dekker'', Gull's Hornbook, p. 163.}}}}

'''totalisation, totaliser,''' etc. See ''totalization'', etc.

'''totality''' (tō-tal′i-ti), ''n.'' [= F. ''totalité'' = Pr. ''totalitat'' = Sp. ''totalidad'' = Pg. ''totalidade'' = It. ''totalità'', < ML. ''totalita(t-)s'', < ''totalis'', total: see ''total.''] 1. The state or character of being a total; entirety.

{{smaller block|There was no handle of weakness to take hold of her by; she was as unseizable, except in her ''totality'', as a billiard-ball.
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', Professor, iii.}}}}

2. That which is total; a whole; an aggregate.

{{smaller block|We must love him with all our heart, mind, and soul; with a threefold ''totality''.
{{right|''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, III. 256.}}}}

{{smaller block|It is absolutely impossible to explain a living or, indeed, a self-efficient ''totality'' of any kind by means of the aggregation of elementary constituents or forces.
{{right|''E. Montgomery'', Mind, IX. 370.}}}}

3. In ''astron.'', the period during which an eclipse is total; the time of total obscuration.

{{smaller block|The coppery hue after the commencement of ''totality'' was of a duller tint than usual.
{{right|''Athenæum'', Feb. 4, 1888, p. 150.}}}}

'''totalization''' (tō″tal-i-zā′shon), ''n.'' [< ''totalize'' + ''-ation.''] The act or process of totalizing, or the state of being totalized. Also spelled ''totalisation''.
